# Independent review: bounded Pages status transport

Verdict: no remaining blocking finding in the scoped status transport. The four final source snapshots match Git commit 3a77e27 byte-for-byte.

Reviewed transport.py, the isolated worker, six tests and the updated README against the receipt invariant and chapter 15 distinction between operation status and current publication. Production connects directly to the fixed api.github.com host with the standard HTTPSConnection certificate/hostname checks, no alternate endpoint, proxy lookup or redirect handler. The receipt is revalidated, credential characters are constrained, the API version is pinned, and authentication travels to the worker through stdin rather than argv. Only a bounded base64 body is returned and parsed again by the parent.

Resolved independent finding: an HTTP 200 response declaring Content-Length 120 but closing after a complete 20-byte success JSON was initially accepted as SUCCEEDED. truncated-repro.py/json preserve the actual loopback-server counterexample. Explicit framing and length validation now rejects that same response, along with invalid/duplicate lengths or length-plus-transfer-encoding ambiguity. truncated-final evidence records the corrected rejection.

Independent execution: all six transport tests passed normally and under Python -O. They include actual loopback HTTP parsing, redirects/status/MIME/content-encoding/body/JSON rejection, sanitized connection errors, invalid request rejection, parent IPC validation, and a real sleeping subprocess terminated at its configured timeout. A separate raw HTTP server probe confirms valid chunked input and the exact 65,536-byte response pass; missing chunk termination, duplicate/unknown transfer encodings and 65,537 bytes fail. Another probe runs worker.main in real isolated Python processes with only its low-level transport replaced inside the test: valid input returns just the base64 body; malformed/oversized input returns nonzero with empty stdout/stderr. commands.json and raw logs preserve all final executions.

The README timeout claim was corrected after review. Python kills and waits for a timed-out child, but process creation may be uninterruptible and cleanup/scheduling add overhead; the configured wait is not a strict end-to-end realtime guarantee. See [Python subprocess.run](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html#subprocess.run). The default TLS behavior was checked against [Python HTTPSConnection](https://docs.python.org/3/library/http.client.html#http.client.HTTPSConnection).

Scope limits: the HTTP tests replace HTTPSConnection with loopback HTTP, and the worker probe replaces network behavior. No real TLS handshake, certificate rejection, GitHub authentication or live status request was exercised. Source inspection establishes default TLS configuration, not interoperability evidence. Status succeed still does not prove current publication, journal freshness, payload identity, public smoke, LKG promotion or deployment authorization. This module performs no retry, cancellation or deployment. The reviewer made no production edits and does not infer full publisher acceptance from these tests.
